2. User Roles & Permissions
The platform has four distinct roles with strictly enforced access control via Spring Security's @PreAuthorize annotations.

2.1 Candidate
The person being referred for a job.
•	Register, verify email, complete profile (photo, skills, experience, LinkedIn)
•	Upload resume PDF (stored in AWS S3 / Cloudinary)
•	Browse open job listings
•	Send referral requests to employees at a company, attaching their resume and a message
•	Chat with the employee via WebSocket before the referral is submitted
•	View referral status dashboard — who referred them, for which job, and current status
•	Receive email notification on every status change
•	Cannot see other candidates, other referrals, or any HR data

2.2 Employee (Referrer)
A company employee who refers candidates they know or who request referrals.
•	Browse open job listings at their company
•	Receive referral requests from candidates — see their profile, resume, and message
•	Chat with candidate via WebSocket to assess their fit
•	Accept request and submit formal referral (or decline with a reason)
•	Directly refer a candidate they already know without waiting for a request
•	View personal referral dashboard — all referrals submitted, their current status
•	Get notified when their referred candidate is hired (for bonus tracking)
•	Cannot see other employees' referrals or any HR pipeline data

2.3 HR Admin
Company HR who receives and processes referrals submitted through the platform.
•	View all referrals submitted for their company in a paginated table
•	Filter by job, status, date range, and referring employee
•	Open any referral — view full candidate profile, resume download, recommendation note
•	Move referral through the 4 platform statuses: SUBMITTED → UNDER REVIEW → ACCEPTED → HIRED / NOT SELECTED
•	Add internal notes at each stage change
•	Every status change triggers email to both candidate and referring employee
•	View analytics dashboard — referral volume, conversion rate, top referrers, avg. time to outcome
•	Cannot manage users, create jobs, or access other companies' data

2.4 Super Admin
Platform owner. Controls the entire RefNet instance.
•	Create and manage company accounts on the platform
•	Manage job listings — create, edit, close, delete for any company
•	Manage all users — change roles, deactivate, reset passwords
•	View master analytics dashboard across all companies
•	View full immutable audit log (stored in MongoDB) — every action with timestamp and actor
•	Optional: impersonate any user for debugging purposes

3. Referral Lifecycle
RefNet tracks a referral through exactly 4 statuses. Anything beyond ACCEPTED is the company's internal ATS responsibility.

Status	Meaning	Trigger
SUBMITTED	Employee has submitted the referral	Candidate + Employee get email
UNDER REVIEW	HR has seen it, reviewing internally	Candidate + Employee get email
ACCEPTED	HR confirms candidate is being processed	Candidate + Employee get email
HIRED / NOT SELECTED	Final outcome — HR updates this once known	Candidate + Employee get email + bonus flag set

Why only 4 statuses?
Because RefNet is a referral platform, not an ATS. Detailed pipeline stages (screening, technical round, HR round) belong to the company's Workday/Greenhouse/internal system.
This is a deliberate architectural decision — mention it explicitly in interviews.

4. System Architecture
4.1 Architecture Pattern — Modular Monolith
RefNet is built as a Modular Monolith — a single Spring Boot application structured with clear domain boundaries. Each domain (Auth, User, Referral, Notification, Analytics) is an independent package with its own controllers, services, repositories, and DTOs. This means:
•	It runs as one deployable unit (simple, no infrastructure overhead)
•	Each domain is loosely coupled — could be extracted into a microservice later
•	In interviews, say: 'I chose modular monolith over microservices because the team size and scale don't justify the operational complexity of microservices. The domain boundaries are clean enough to extract later if needed.'

4.2 Request Flow
Every HTTP request flows through the following layers in sequence:
1.	Browser / Mobile Client sends HTTP request
2.	CORS Filter — allows cross-origin requests from frontend domain
3.	JWT Auth Filter — validates token, extracts user identity and role
4.	Spring Security — checks @PreAuthorize on the controller method
5.	Controller — receives request, validates DTO with @Valid
6.	Service Layer — business logic, calls repository
7.	Repository (JPA / MongoRepository) — queries database
8.	Response — DTO returned as JSON, never the raw Entity

4.3 Module Breakdown
Module	Responsibility
auth-module	Register, login, JWT generation, refresh token, email verification, password reset
user-module	User profiles, roles, profile photo, resume upload, search
job-module	Job listings CRUD, open/close status, company scoping
referral-module	Referral request, referral submission, status transitions, notes
chat-module	WebSocket STOMP config, message persistence, conversation threads
notification-module	Email sending (async), in-app notification storage, read/unread tracking
analytics-module	Aggregation queries, stats endpoints, chart data endpoints
