8. WebSocket Architecture
8.1 Why WebSocket for Chat
HTTP is a request-response protocol. The client always initiates. For chat, the server needs to PUSH a message to the receiver the moment it arrives — HTTP cannot do this without polling (client asks every N seconds — wasteful and slow).
WebSocket opens a persistent TCP connection between client and server. Either side can send data at any time. This is the correct protocol for any real-time feature.

8.2 STOMP on top of WebSocket
Raw WebSocket is a low-level byte pipe — it has no concept of destinations, routing, or pub/sub. STOMP (Simple Text Oriented Messaging Protocol) adds:
•	SUBSCRIBE to a destination (channel)
•	SEND to a destination
•	MESSAGE pushed from server to subscribers
Think of WebSocket as a road and STOMP as the traffic rules on that road.

8.3 Implementation
// Server config (Spring Boot)
@Configuration @EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class WebSocketConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {

  @Override public void registerStompEndpoints(StompEndpointRegistry r) {
    r.addEndpoint('/ws').setAllowedOriginPatterns('*').withSockJS();
  }

  @Override public void configureMessageBroker(MessageBrokerRegistry r) {
    r.enableSimpleBroker('/user', '/topic');
    r.setApplicationDestinationPrefixes('/app');
    r.setUserDestinationPrefix('/user');
  }
}

// When employee sends message, server delivers to candidate's private queue:
messagingTemplate.convertAndSendToUser(
  receiverId, '/queue/messages', new MessageDTO(...)
);

// Frontend (JavaScript)
const socket = new SockJS('/ws');
const stompClient = Stomp.over(socket);
stompClient.connect({'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + token}, () => {
  stompClient.subscribe('/user/queue/messages', (msg) => {
    const message = JSON.parse(msg.body);
    renderNewMessage(message);  // instant, no polling
  });
});

8.4 Message Persistence
Every WebSocket message sent through the platform is ALSO saved to the messages table in PostgreSQL. This ensures:
•	Chat history is available when the user refreshes or reconnects
•	Messages are not lost if the receiver is offline — they load history on next login
•	The chat endpoint GET /messages/conversations/{id} returns full history

9. Security Architecture
9.1 JWT Flow
9.	User logs in with email + password
10.	Server verifies password against BCrypt hash in DB
11.	Server issues: Access Token (JWT, 15 min expiry) + Refresh Token (UUID, 7 days, stored in DB)
12.	Access Token returned in response body. Refresh Token set as HttpOnly cookie
13.	Frontend stores Access Token in memory (NOT localStorage — XSS vulnerable)
14.	Every API request sends: Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
15.	JWT Filter validates token signature, expiry, and extracts userId + role
16.	When Access Token expires, frontend calls POST /auth/refresh — server validates Refresh Token cookie and issues new Access Token
17.	Logout: server deletes Refresh Token from DB. Old Access Tokens are short-lived so they expire naturally.

Interview talking point:
Access token in memory (not localStorage) prevents XSS attacks from stealing tokens.
Refresh token in HttpOnly cookie prevents JavaScript access — CSRF protection via SameSite=Strict cookie attribute.
Short access token lifetime (15 min) limits the damage window if a token is ever leaked.

9.2 Role-Based Access Control
Every controller method has @PreAuthorize enforcing the minimum required role:
@GetMapping('/referrals')
@PreAuthorize("hasAnyRole('HR_ADMIN', 'SUPER_ADMIN')")
public ResponseEntity<Page<ReferralDTO>> getAllReferrals(...) { ... }

@PatchMapping('/referrals/{id}/status')
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('HR_ADMIN')")
public ResponseEntity<ReferralDTO> updateStatus(...) { ... }

9.3 Additional Security Measures
•	Rate limiting on login endpoint — max 5 failed attempts, then 15-minute lockout (using Redis counter)
•	Email verification required before first login
•	Password reset tokens expire in 1 hour
•	File upload validation — only PDF allowed for resumes, max 5MB, MIME type checked server-side
•	All inputs validated with @Valid and custom validators
•	SQL injection impossible — JPA parameterised queries only
•	CORS configured to allow only the frontend domain
•	Secrets (DB passwords, JWT secret, S3 keys) only via environment variables — never in source code

10. Async Processing & Notifications
10.1 Why Async
When HR changes a referral status, the API must:
•	Update the database
•	Send an email to the candidate
•	Send an email to the referring employee
•	Create in-app notifications for both
If all of this is done synchronously in the HTTP thread, the API response takes 2-3 seconds (SMTP is slow). The user sees a frozen UI. This is unacceptable.
With @Async, the API updates the DB and returns a response in ~50ms. The emails and notifications are processed in a background thread pool — the user never waits.

10.2 Implementation
@Service
public class NotificationService {

  @Async('notificationExecutor')
  public void sendStatusChangeNotification(Referral referral, String newStatus) {
    // This runs in background thread — HTTP response already sent
    emailService.sendToCandidate(referral, newStatus);
    emailService.sendToReferrer(referral, newStatus);
    inAppNotificationService.createForBoth(referral, newStatus);
  }
}

// Thread pool config
@Bean('notificationExecutor')
public Executor notificationExecutor() {
  ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
  executor.setCorePoolSize(4);
  executor.setMaxPoolSize(10);
  executor.setQueueCapacity(500);
  return executor;
}

10.3 In-App Notification Bell
The notification bell in the navbar shows an unread count badge. This is implemented via simple REST polling — NOT WebSocket.
Frontend calls GET /notifications/unread-count every 30 seconds. The count is cached in Redis (invalidated when a new notification is created). The DB is rarely hit. This is efficient and simple — no WebSocket overhead for a count number.

