12. Concurrency — Multi-Device Support
You mentioned testing with 2-3 devices simultaneously. Here is how the platform handles this correctly.

12.1 Stateless Backend
Because the backend is stateless (JWT-based auth, no server-side session), any number of devices can be logged in with the same or different accounts simultaneously with no conflict. Each request carries its own JWT — the server processes them independently.

12.2 Database Concurrency
PostgreSQL handles concurrent writes with MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control). Two users updating different records simultaneously — no issue. Two users updating the same record — PostgreSQL serializes with row-level locking. The last writer wins, which is acceptable for this use case.
For referral status updates, optimistic locking is used:
@Entity
public class Referral {
  @Version
  private Long version;  // JPA detects concurrent modification
}

12.3 WebSocket Multi-Device
If a user is logged into two devices simultaneously, both devices have WebSocket connections open. When a message arrives, Spring's user-destination routing delivers it to ALL active sessions for that user — both devices receive it. This is exactly how WhatsApp Web works.

12.4 Redis Cache Invalidation
If one device triggers an action that invalidates a cache (e.g., HR admin adds a job on device A), the Redis cache for job listings is cleared immediately. Device B's next API call fetches fresh data from PostgreSQL and repopulates the cache.

