15. Resume & Interview Summary
15.1 Resume Project Description
RefNet — Job Referral Management Platform
Java · Spring Boot 3 · Spring Security · PostgreSQL · MongoDB · Redis · WebSocket · Docker

Built a full-stack referral platform with JWT authentication (access + refresh tokens), role-based
access control (Candidate, Employee, HR Admin, Super Admin), and real-time chat using WebSocket
with STOMP protocol. Features async email notifications via JavaMailSender with a dedicated thread
pool, Redis caching for high-read endpoints, polyglot persistence (PostgreSQL + MongoDB for audit
logs), resume upload to AWS S3, and an analytics dashboard with Chart.js. Containerized with Docker
Compose and deployed on Railway with a GitHub Actions CI/CD pipeline. REST API documented with
Swagger/OpenAPI. Designed as a modular monolith with clean domain boundaries.

15.2 Key Interview Talking Points
Question	Your Answer
Why modular monolith over microservices?	Team size and scale don't justify microservices overhead. Clean domain boundaries allow future extraction.
Why only 4 referral statuses?	RefNet is a referral platform, not an ATS. The hiring pipeline is the company's concern. Deliberate decoupling.
Why WebSocket only for chat?	Chat needs real-time push. Status change notifications are async — email is sufficient and correct here.
Why access token in memory, not localStorage?	localStorage is XSS-vulnerable. Memory storage + HttpOnly cookie for refresh token is the secure pattern.
Why MongoDB for audit logs?	Audit events are unstructured, append-only documents. NoSQL is the natural fit — no schema migrations needed.
Why Redis for caching?	Job listings are read far more than written. Redis reduces DB load and improves response time on read-heavy endpoints.
How does concurrency work?	Stateless JWT backend handles multiple devices. Optimistic locking prevents concurrent write conflicts. WebSocket delivers to all active sessions.
Why async notifications?	SMTP is slow I/O. Synchronous sending would block HTTP threads and degrade API performance.

16. Recommended Build Order
Build in this exact order so every layer is testable before building the next.

18.	Project setup — Spring Boot init, dependencies, folder structure, Docker Compose
19.	Database schema — PostgreSQL tables + indexes, MongoDB connection
20.	Auth module — register, email verification, login, JWT, refresh token
21.	User module — profile CRUD, resume upload (local file storage first, S3 later)
22.	Job module — job listings CRUD
23.	Referral request flow — candidate sends request, employee accepts/declines
24.	Referral submission flow — employee submits referral, HR views and updates status
25.	Notification module — async email + in-app notifications
